well german chancellor angela merkel has  said germany will leave afghanistan with  other coalition forces on the 31st of  august the announcement came as part of  the chancellor's address to the german  parliament as a government seeks  retroactive approval for its military  mission in afghanistan the largest in  bundesvare history opposition parties  have heavily criticized the government's  handling of the withdrawal  and chancellor merkel are called on  western countries to continue their  support for local support staff even  after coalition troops have gone  the end of the airlift which u.s  president joe biden  adhered to he didn't give us an  extension yesterday  means that this will be the end of our  efforts and that will mean  supporting the afghan  staff that are even more desperate now  than they were when the taliban first  took over  and we must protect them  and this means that we are working  intensely in the days remaining us  to find ways  to continue to help those who helped us  to offer protection  not least through civilian operations of  the airport in kabul and it's also about  offering humanitarian support  for those who need this help more than  ever  well let's get more on the chancellor  merkel's speech to the german parliament  with the w chief political editor  michaela kufner who is there at the  bundestag welcome michaela despite  admitting that quotes we were all wrong  about the speed of the taliban victory  end quote the chancellor was in no mood  to apologize  no she wasn't she actually said that the  initial aim had been achieved which was  to prevent any terrorist attack to start  from afghanistan's ground once again  although of course that we're heading  into new territory there all together  with is um already being reported to  arrive at the scene um she said she  would do several things now some 600  million euros have been earmarked to for  humanitarian assess assistance through  u.n agencies but also to support  countries in the region not to hand over  to a taliban government that she made  clear also germany would continue to try  and get as many people out as possible  and yes it wants to talk to the taliban  and get guarantees and to somehow  safeguard people who are still stuck  there but that is a little um to really  um help um calm those fears right now  with the end of this evacuation of  course being imminent  and she directly addressed the  criticisms that the german evacuation  had begun too late  yes she said it's easy to now  say that  what would have been better in the past  she spoke of difficult choices that  needed to be made  she said that one had to decide whether  to weaken the government by already  pulling people out there were great  fears amongst the previous government  one can say now that this would start a  panic but the fact is that germans were  amongst the last to realize how  dangerous the situation was even in  evacuating their own staff and there is  a painstaking search now for where the  key mistake was made but what is clear  is that individual ministries were kind  of pushing responsibility towards each  other and that doesn't seem to measure  up to the magnitude of this collective  failure here  so more questions being raised and if we  weren't just four weeks away from the  general election here in germany  i dare say that angela mackel remaining  in  office would even be in doubt but with  those elections just a few weeks away  the interesting thing here will be what  people's will people will vote for when  they go to the ballot box with  afghanistan german foreign policy now  clearly an election issue  and i wanted to pick up on that point so  germans do see that this crisis in  afghanistan as being relevant to next  month's election  absolutely and there seems to be um  there's  been actually demonstrations in the  streets here uh as well um or not huge  ones but um people do feel a  responsibility towards um those people  who were trying